 "text": "The Táin bó Cuailnge is an Irish epic. It is the greatest surviving piece of Irish mythological literature. The tale tells of a great cattle raid upon the region now known as the Cooley peninsula. For that reason the piece is sometimes anglicized as the Cattle Raid of Cooley. It belongs to the Ulster Cycle of myths. Christian monks at the monastery of Clonmacnoise recorded it in the Middle Ages. Its material is older than that copy. Bards had passed the tale down by mouth for centuries before it was written. Several fore-tales describe the causes of the raid. One tells of the rivalry between Queen Medb of Connacht and her husband Ailill. Ailill owned one bull more than Medb did. The great White Bull had been born in her herds but went to his lands. Under Brehon law her status as wife hung on her wealth. So Medb set out to match her husband. The equal of the White Bull lived in Ulster on the Cuailnge peninsula. Medb offered its lord, Daire mac Fiachna, gold and her own favor for a one-year loan. He agreed at first. Then he heard her warriors boast they would take the bull with or without his leave. He broke the deal. Medb sailed out to steal the beast. The women of Ulster lay under a curse laid by Macha. At attack their warriors fell into pain like labor. Medb marched off-season toward Samhain, when the warriors could not fight. The armies set out from Crugan in good heart. The hero Cuculainn was not of Ulster, so he stood ready to fight. He drew the invaders on and slew them one by one. The goddess Morrigan came to help Medb but could not stop him. While Cuculainn fought, Medb slipped past and stole the brown bull Donn. The men of Ulster then recovered from the curse. They overtook her army but lost the prize. Medb carried the bull back to Connacht. The white bull Finnbennach and the brown Donn then fought as they always had. The brown slew the white and died of the strain. A late tale says the epic was part lost, then restored by the son of the chief bard. That bard called up the ghost of the warrior Fergus, who had joined the raid. The ghost recited the missing tale before the king's court. The poet who summoned the bard fell dead at the end.",
 "okr": {
  "objective": "Relate the epic of the Tain of Cow-Cooley, the Ulster cattle raid.",
